      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the technical aspects.\n\nThe knocking sound in your electric water heater is likely caused by a faulty or loose heat exchanger, which is a critical component that transfers heat from the heating element to the water. Over time, the heat exchanger can become corroded, damaged, or loose, causing vibrations and noise.\n\nAnother possible cause is a buildup of sediment or debris in the heat exchanger, which can also lead to vibrations and knocking sounds. This sediment can be caused by mineral buildup, dirt, or other contaminants that can accumulate in the system.\n\nIn some cases, the knocking sound can also be caused by a faulty thermostat or a malfunctioning temperature sensor, which can cause the heating element to overheat or underheat, leading to vibrations and noise.\n\nIt's worth noting that the knocking sound can also be a sign of a more serious issue, such as a cracked or damaged heat exchanger, which can lead to a complete failure of the water heater.\n\nAs for the lukewarm water, it's likely that the heat exchanger is not functioning properly, or that there's a blockage in the system. A plumber can inspect the heat exchanger and other",
